All right.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.280
Just a little bit
of a pulse check.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.480
Who is here today?

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.892
Who is an engineer in the room?

00:02:04.892 --> 00:02:06.010
All right, the majority.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.880
That's my audience.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.740
CXO, ITDM?

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.660
OK, so most engineers.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.740
So what are the criterias
that I go through today

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.390
when I'm looking at some of the
products of the Google Cloud

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:20.970
platform?

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.450
So first of all, I'm going
to look at performance.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.040
And performance,
what I understand

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.320
under that is basically
the response time,

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.440
like how quickly an
application responds.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.540
Especially if you are
in the upper layers

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.840
of your application, you want
to have things being snappy.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.750
So you want to have very
low latencies in terms

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:43.490
of round trip times and load
processing times, so to say.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:46.280
It's especially important
in human interactions.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.920
They don't want to wait for a
second or even half a second

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.560
until something loads up, right?

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.170
Second point, which is important
for me, is reliability.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.665
So what happens if there's
something that fails?

00:02:56.665 --> 00:02:58.370
Is there a failover?

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.920
Is there some
redundancy there that

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.530
can help me to have
my application still

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:07.340
up and running, even if
parts of my application fail?

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.830
The third part, and that
is part of the title,

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:11.510
is the scalability.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:14.990
So what if-- what happens
if my application goes

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.780
viral and my user base grows?

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.330
Do I have to
re-architect completely?

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.180
Do I have to change system,
change products that I'm using?

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.980
Or can I just use them from
user zero to user one billion?

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.160
And last but not least,
also part of the title,

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:32.090
are the costs.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:33.860
Now, costs are
not only the costs

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.410
that you get charged
on your credit card,

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:40.430
or if you're having offline
billing with us installed,

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.910
you have to pay per
bank wire, probably.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.410
Not only these costs
are considered.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:48.080
You should also consider
costs like migration costs,

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.210
costs of operation.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.730
What is the human
labor that your company

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.520
has to put in to manage
certain systems, right?

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.350
So, yes, there are
systems out there

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:58.970
that are cheaper than others.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.980
And it looks like,
why would I pay more?

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.530
But the labor cost adds up.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.950
And so you should
consider that as well.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.440
That's why you see in the cost
section not only the money

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:09.940
back.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.720
You see also the
people involved there.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.830
And I will make a couple of
statements along those lines

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:16.730
along this talk.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:18.829
All right, so what
do I understand

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:20.860
on a full-stack application?

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.330
You saw it in the
agenda already.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.600
So I, for myself, consider a
full-stack application being

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.040
a presentation layer--

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:30.580
so basically assets
that I serve--

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.330
then an application
layer, which basically

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.960
has my business logic and
processing, and a persistence

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.840
layer, which is basically my
storage, a database, things

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.130
like that.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.880
On the side, I have on to one
side service APIs, so things

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.680
that you can call out into the
Google Cloud Platform, or even

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.150
other providers,
or even on premise.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.020
And on the right
side, you have logging

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.050
and analytics,
which is integrated

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:58.260
in a lot of our products.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.860
But you can also integrate
that into your applications

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.210
that you're writing.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.960
All right, so let's start
with the presentation layer.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.210
So my choices of
presentation layer--

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.070
I'm a big, big fan of Firebase.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:13.630
Firebase makes it really, really
easy to start coding, getting

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:14.740
something up and running.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.920
And part of the Firebase
platform and ecosystem

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.930
is the Firebase hosting.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:21.460
So you see on the
right side there,

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.300
you have a portfolio of things
that you get out of the box

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:29.320
with Firebase, being
authentication, a database--

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:31.530
a NoSQL database, Firestore--

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.700
blob storage, cloud
storage, cloud functions

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:39.270
for processing, and hosting
for hosting static assets.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.040
Now, it's very easy to
get started with Firebase.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.710
But Firebase has also very
simple cost structures.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.470
And so that might
mean at some point,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:49.650
if you are at a certain
scale, that there

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.670
are more affordable
solutions out there for you

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:53.800
to save some costs.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.570
So my second choice
here is, especially

00:05:55.570 --> 00:06:00.490
if you're looking at scale,
cloud storage and cloud CDN.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:04.720
So what am I looking at in
terms of the four criterias

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.230
that I mentioned before?

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:10.690
So if you look at
performance, we're good.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:11.800
They are snappy.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.520
CDNs, Firebase hosting
is also backed by a CDN.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.710
So they are basically
at the edge points

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.590
which we have around the world
for Google Cloud platform.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:22.240
So low latencies, great.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.482
Reliability, static assets
are easy to replicate. right?

00:06:26.482 --> 00:06:28.440
So you cache them everywhere
in the edge cases.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.020
Even if one of your edges falls
down-- or multiple, even--

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.740
they can be fetched
from other edges.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:37.270
So we have replication, so
we are also reliable on that.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:42.070
Scalability, again, you
don't have strong consistency

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:44.470
constrains with static assets.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:49.690
If you have higher ones, you
can set your cache timeout,

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:53.800
basically, low so that the cache
is refreshed more frequently.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.340
But basically, since it's static
assets that we can replicate,

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:59.470
scalability is not
an issue, either.

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:02.110
And when it comes to
cost, I mentioned already

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:04.570
Firebase is kind of
like a static cost.

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:08.890
And Cloud CDN, Cloud
Storage are a little lower.

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:10.870
So what does it mean in detail?

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:13.870
What are the cost drivers
for my presentation layout?

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:16.365
So one of the big costs
drivers is egress.

00:07:16.365 --> 00:07:18.310
So egress is basically
all the bytes

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:22.000
that we ship out of the Google
network to your clients,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.480
to your customers.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.780
These are the things where
you have to look into.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:27.910
And they become--
again, in Firebase,

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:30.090
you have basically a flat rate.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:31.750
For a small installation,
that is fine.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.960
There will be a low difference
between the solution

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:35.560
that you use.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:39.790
But if you go larger
scale, then egress actually

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.460
goes down depending on
the traffic that you have.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.650
So increased traffic,
you get, basically,

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:48.100
down in what you pay per
gigabyte for your egress.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.620
And that only gets--

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:52.300
you can only benefit
from that if you

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.370
use Google Cloud Storage.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.960
Now, if you want to go
even lower in your prices

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:58.510
that you pay for
egress, then you

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:02.580
can use a CDN in front
of Cloud Storage, which

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:03.925
incurs a fill up cost.

00:08:03.925 --> 00:08:06.590
So you have to basically shift
some data into the cache.

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:07.600
You pay that.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.460
But then the egress cost
from the CDNs is quite a bit

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.510
lower than if you compare it,
for instance, with serving data

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.320
directly from Cloud Storage.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.375
So if you are large scale,
please look into Cloud CDN.

00:08:22.375 --> 00:08:24.290
And in front of
Google Cloud Storage,

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.510
this will be much
more affordable

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.750
than if you look at
standard Firebase

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:32.230
or just using Cloud Storage.

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:35.700
Now, the second one is
my application layer.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:39.480
And for the application layer, I
chose three kind of categories.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:43.420
So Cloud Functions
is available on GCP--

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:45.020
that's on Google
Cloud Platform--

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.200
as well as under the Firebase
umbrella and branding.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.530
So you can use it directly from
your Firebase applications.

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:54.590
You can trigger them from HTTP.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.060
You can direct them in
Firebase, for instance,

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:01.040
if you have any data changes
in your NoSQL database,

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:03.530
in the real-time database,
or in Firestore that

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:05.631
is available under Firebase.

00:09:05.631 --> 00:09:07.880
So if you have any data
changes, you can automatically

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.890
trigger your Cloud Function.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:12.110
Cloud Function under
GCP, you can also

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:14.710
trigger when, for instance,
on a Pub/Sub message.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:17.660
So Pub/Sub is our
messaging system,

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.130
which is scalable beyond
belief, at least my belief.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.690
You don't have to do any kind
of sizing or anything decisions.

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:29.085
You just pump data
in there and it

00:09:29.085 --> 00:09:31.460
will arrive at the destination
where you want to have it.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:34.844
And that is globally as well.

00:09:34.844 --> 00:09:36.260
So that is also a
trigger that you

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:37.490
can use for Cloud Functions.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:38.990
And another trigger
that you can use

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:41.730
is any kind of
storage operations.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:44.150
So for instance, if you
use Cloud Storage, which

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:48.390
is our blob storage, if you
store a file or modify a file,

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:49.880
you can also tweak
a Cloud Function

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.510
to do any kind of
post-processing if you want to.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:55.370
The second choice for
my application layer

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:56.390
is App Engine.

00:09:56.390 --> 00:10:01.060
So App Engine has a couple of
really nifty and nice features.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.230
It's scaling down to
zero, for instance.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:05.570
It's flexibly scaling.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:08.660
So there are some
comparisons that you

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:10.860
can draw between Cloud
Functions and App Engine.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.980
But there are also
some differences.

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:16.490
And then the third choice is
basically Kubernetes engine,

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:18.230
which is a running container.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:20.200
So it is our managed Kubernetes.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.240
Kubernetes is a
container orchestrator

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.310
where you can run
your containers

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:30.170
and Kubernetes takes care of
restarting them if they fail.

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:31.950
It's taking care
of node management,

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.830
resource management,
things like that.

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:36.920
You can install a service
mesh in Kubernetes

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.980
to have discoverability,
have security in there,

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:41.490
things like that.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:44.465
So there are a lot of things
that you do manage yourself

00:10:44.465 --> 00:10:46.840
in Kubernetes which are managed
for you in, for instance,

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.900
App Engine and Cloud Function.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:51.080
So let's have a look
at our four criterias.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.410
I know they all look green.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:58.280
I have a couple that are not
green, just to mention that.

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.260
But basically, if you
look at Cloud Functions,

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:03.940
like performance, reliability,
so Cloud Functions,

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:06.470
unless it's the very first
call, which takes a little bit

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:07.530
of a warm-up--

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:11.060
but if you have follow up calls,
then they are really snappy.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:17.310
They're in low tens of
milliseconds, depending,

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:19.550
of course, on your processing,
how much processing

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:21.080
you do in your Cloud Function.

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:23.690
But if you, for instance,
just return a "hello, world,"

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:26.990
you can get into the tens of
milliseconds in response time.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.960
In terms of reliability,
Cloud Functions

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.170
is running on our
infrastructure.

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:35.060
It's also you
basically-- you just

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:37.250
can scale very
easily in containers.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:40.850
And if one falls down, we
can just reroute the traffic

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:43.170
to another one that is live.

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:46.850
And they are in a region,
so they can be brought up

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:48.690
in different zones.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:50.840
So even if a zone
becomes unavailable,

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:52.790
your Cloud Function
is still available.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:56.080
In terms of scalability, I
mentioned, as I say there,

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:57.200
order of seconds.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.386
So if you have to warm up new
instances of your container,

00:12:02.386 --> 00:12:03.760
which runs the
Cloud Function, it

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:07.680
is usually at the order
of very low second range.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:11.000
Now, for App Engine, it's
a very similar story.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.010
So App Engine has
certain constraints

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:14.480
in terms of processing.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:16.680
If you have a front
end processing job,

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.580
it's constrained to 60 seconds.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:22.520
After 60 seconds, we
basically time it out.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:25.280
In Cloud Functions, you have
nine minutes to process.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.100
After nine minutes, we basically
stop the function processing.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:30.680
So these are boundaries
that you have

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:33.540
to consider if you do certain
kinds of business logic.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:36.770
For App Engine, we have
our own processes which

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:38.294
are allowed to run much longer.

00:12:38.294 --> 00:12:39.960
I don't have the exact
number right now,

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.830
but they're allowed to
run much longer if you

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:46.820
need to do some asynchronous
background processing.

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:49.850
Now, for Kubernetes, if
you're inside the Cloud store,

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:52.290
you still have
resources available.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.980
Then you can scale at the
order of milliseconds.

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:57.770
So bringing up a
port is pretty fast,

00:12:57.770 --> 00:13:02.520
unless you do a lot of warm-up
initialization in your port.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:07.250
But I'm a big fan, for
instance, of writing Golang apps

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:08.620
nowadays.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:11.300
And I statically
link these binaries

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:12.680
and put them in
my container that

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:14.930
makes the containers very
small, so even if they have

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:17.300
to be fetched from a registry.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:20.690
And then they are started
up in milliseconds,

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:22.200
really, really, really quick.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.520
Now, if you run out of sources
in your Kubernetes engine

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.770
cluster, you need to
scale the cluster.

00:13:27.770 --> 00:13:31.500
And that is basically adding
Compute Engine instances.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.250
And that is at the
order of minutes.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:36.120
So it takes about 30 seconds
to bring up a Compute Engine

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:36.950
instance.

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:39.120
And then it takes about
another 30 seconds

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:44.340
to basically set up the
whole Kubernetes stack which

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:47.020
we need to manage that node.

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:52.050
Now, the advantage
of Kubernetes is also

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:54.720
you don't have any kind of
runtime restrictions there.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:57.060
So you basically
run your container.

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:58.950
It can run whatever
you want in there.

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:01.970
It basically runs
on the Linux kernel.

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:03.630
You can run your
container on there

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:06.660
and you don't have restrictions,
like how long it can run

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:09.040
and it gets stopped,
or things like that.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.360
Now what are cost drivers
for Google Cloud Function, so

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:13.800
a GCF, Google Cloud Function?

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.802
GAE is Google App Engine.

00:14:15.802 --> 00:14:18.660
GKE is Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:20.130
What are the cost drivers here?

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:23.190
So for functions, you have,
basically, an instance type

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:25.130
that you can choose.

00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:29.190
It's memory and
processing power-based,

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.260
so CPU hertz-based.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:35.970
You see there, it's from 128
megabytes to two gigabyte.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:37.660
Based on the size
that you choose,

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:43.260
you have a certain cost per
second for that instance.

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:45.210
And then it's basically
also a call count.

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:47.830
Depending on how
many calls you have,

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:50.940
we basically time how long
your function is running,

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:54.810
and then we calculate
the cost there.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:57.860
All of those, what's coming
in is the egress as well.

00:14:57.860 --> 00:15:00.210
Now, the advantage of
the egress for these ones

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:03.090
is that for Cloud
Functions it's a baseline.

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:05.220
So you basically pay
the same no matter

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:09.840
if you call it once a day or
100,000 times or a million

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.000
times a day.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:15.090
For App Engine, it's the same.

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:18.150
Now, for Kubernetes
Engine, we price the egress

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:21.330
based on our standard
networking prices.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:23.130
And the networking
prices is based

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.800
in the sense of that the
more traffic you have,

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:28.140
the lower the price
goes for egress.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:31.740
So consider that if you
have a big application which

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:36.150
gets viral, your application
will become cheaper over time

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:39.840
the more traffic you get if you
run it on Kubernetes versus App

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.420
Engine or Google Cloud Function.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:44.910
Now, one note to that,
if you run Kubernetes,

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:46.910
even if you run
Kubernetes Engine,

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:49.290
the managing of your
applications on Kubernetes

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:51.950
will incur labor costs.

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:53.520
So you have to consider that.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:55.720
Now, yes, you have that
also with App Engine.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:57.350
You also have that
with Functions.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.510
But there are a little
bit of differences

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:02.130
in the magnitude, how much
management, labor cost

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:04.170
management is there.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:07.620
Now, for the sizes,
basically, if you

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:13.710
have jobs which require a lot
of memory or a lot of CPU power,

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:17.010
you are out of luck, pretty
much, in GCF or in GAE.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:19.535
They are capped
at-- for Google App

00:16:19.535 --> 00:16:22.590
Engine at one gigabyte
memory, for GCF at two,

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:26.100
and at 2.4 gigahertz
of processing power.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.080
Now, for Kubernetes
Engine, you basically

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.760
can take big machines,
take your 64 core machine

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.400
with two terabytes
of main memory.

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:38.290
And if your port needs a
terabyte of main memory,

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:38.880
so be it.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:40.540
You can do it.

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:41.910
So consider that, as well.

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:45.940
If you have some really
heavyweight processing,

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:49.060
you might want to go more
to a GKE-based system

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:51.360
than something else.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:52.890
All right, number
three, and that

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:57.210
is more-- that is basically
my big passion as a Google

00:16:57.210 --> 00:17:00.510
Developer Advocate for
the Cloud platform,

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:02.190
is the persistence layer.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.190
So now we have a couple
of different types

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:09.558
of persistence products there.

00:17:09.558 --> 00:17:11.599
The first one that you
see here is Cloud Storage.

00:17:11.599 --> 00:17:14.369
So Cloud Storage is
basically a blob store.

00:17:14.369 --> 00:17:17.310
So if you have any kinds of
objects that you want to store,

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:19.170
any kinds of assets,
then Cloud Storage

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:22.380
is what you want to use.

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:26.190
Cloud Firestore is our
NoSQL document, so to say,

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:31.200
document database where you
can store data without having

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:33.720
to pre-define a schema.

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:36.510
And that's very
flexible in terms of it

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:42.260
can grow in terms of processing
as well as data very flexibly.

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:47.790
Cloud SQL, you have basically
a managed MySQL or Postgres.

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:50.700
And Cloud Spanner is our
relational database system

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.770
which is horizontally scalable.

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:54.840
Now, if we look at Cloud
Storage and Firestore

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:58.830
in terms of for reliability,
scalability, performance,

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:00.420
they are pretty snappy.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:01.800
They're very quick.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:03.600
The really nice feature
of Cloud Storage

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:07.210
is that the listing
is strong, consistent.

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:09.840
So if you add a file
to Cloud Storage

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:11.610
and you are at the
other end of the world

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:13.950
and you ask if that
file exists, it

00:18:13.950 --> 00:18:16.590
will give you a strong,
consistent view.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.434
If you look from two
places in the world--

00:18:18.434 --> 00:18:19.350
let's say it this way.

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:21.930
If you look from two places
in the world at roughly

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:25.470
the same time, you will get
the same view of the data

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:26.730
you have in Cloud Storage.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.520
That is not true for
a lot of other systems

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:31.860
that are out there in the world.

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:35.370
In terms of scalability, they're
very scalable, these systems.

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:37.650
You don't have to look
at sizing or anything.

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.410
You basically start
from zero and you grow.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.012
It grows with your application.

00:18:42.012 --> 00:18:43.470
You don't have to
worry about-- you

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:47.780
don't have to give us any sizing
information or any of this.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:51.330
Now, if your app gets
really, really viral--

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:52.770
I don't want to
name the company.

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:55.395
But there are certain companies
that build their whole business

00:18:55.395 --> 00:18:57.450
on App Engine and
Datastore, which is

00:18:57.450 --> 00:18:59.490
the predecessor of Firestore.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:03.720
Now, if you get at that billion
dollar valuation kind of scale,

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:05.970
please still talk to us.

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:09.161
We want to make sure that your
business runs reliable and gets

00:19:09.161 --> 00:19:10.410
the performance that you need.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:15.460
So we give you some
dedicated resources for that.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.670
Now, if you look in the
relational database area,

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:20.920
we have Cloud SQL
and Cloud Spanner.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:25.110
Now, that's where my
first non-green check

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:27.490
marks are coming, or crosses.

00:19:27.490 --> 00:19:33.240
If you look at Cloud SQL,
in terms of reliability,

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:36.610
now Cloud SQL is, out
of the box, basically,

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:37.720
a single host system.

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:41.600
So if this system falls,
down you become unavailable.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:46.100
So you don't have, necessarily,
out-of-the-box replication.

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:47.680
Now, what you can
do with Cloud SQL

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.180
and the Google Cloud
platform is you

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:51.590
can actually set up
a failover instance.

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:55.210
And you can set up read replicas
to get a little bit more

00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:56.840
scalability out of your system.

00:19:56.840 --> 00:20:01.180
But even if we have to failover
to your failover database,

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.200
it will incur little downtime.

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:06.310
It will take some time
to basically catch up

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:09.250
with the logs and
stuff like that.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:12.440
Now, if you look at scalability
for Cloud SQL, for instance,

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:17.380
you can upsize your
Cloud SQL instance.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.900
So you can scale it vertically.

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:23.140
You can jump between
different sizes of instance

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:25.210
sizes between Cloud SQL.

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:27.090
But they are, at some
point, capped, right?

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:30.080
You have a maximum instance
size that we can support.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:31.870
And if you outgrow
that, you're of luck,

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:35.560
pretty much, unless you are
building your application

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:39.370
on top that can manage to
distribute your application

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:43.810
workload across multiple Cloud
SQL instances, for instance.

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:46.870
But even if you
upgrade your instance,

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:50.980
then you are at the order of
at least minutes, if not hours,

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:53.320
to basically do that upgrade.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:56.080
And your customers
can't access or do

00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:58.420
things with your application
during that time.

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:00.670
Now, if we look
at Cloud Spanner,

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:04.120
this was designed
with the idea in mind

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:06.670
to actually eliminate
these boundaries,

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.890
eliminate the
boundaries of scaling,

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:12.730
and eliminating the boundaries
of having maintenance,

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:15.850
even if it's planned
maintenance outages.

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:18.610
Now, Cloud Spanner is still,
even though it gives you

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:21.040
the quality, a
relational database

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:22.790
with strong consistency.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:26.560
So we have strong consistency
across tables, regions,

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.690
and even globally
that you get there.

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:31.220
And you can do
no-downtime updates.

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:34.510
So you can basically add
nodes or remove nodes

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:36.970
from Cloud Spanner,
depending on your workloads.

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:39.740
And you can also
update your schema,

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:42.110
which is running
inside a transaction

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:46.380
as well, even while you're
serving live traffic.

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:51.190
So now what are the cost drivers
for Cloud Storage, Firestore,

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:52.710
SQL, and Spanner?

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:54.880
So for Cloud Storage,
it's basically

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:56.750
the storage and egress.

00:21:56.750 --> 00:22:00.460
For Firestore, you are charged
based on the operations, so

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:02.290
storage and, again, egress.

00:22:02.290 --> 00:22:04.720
You have heard that by
now a couple of times.

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:08.380
Cloud SQL, based on the
instance type that you choose

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:11.290
and if you have a failover
instance and read replicas,

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:13.900
that basically adds
to the cost as well.

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:16.600
So if you want to have high
availability and disaster

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:19.780
recovery, that's basically
what you pay for.

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:22.540
And Cloud Spanner, we charge
you based on the configuration

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:23.090
that you use.

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:25.120
So configuration is
basically, do you

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:26.530
want to have a
regional instance?

00:22:26.530 --> 00:22:29.170
Do you want to have a
multi-region continental

00:22:29.170 --> 00:22:29.740
instance?

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:32.950
Or do you want to have a
multi-region global instance?

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:35.110
Based on that, we
charge different amounts

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:38.627
of money per node that
you provision with us.

00:22:38.627 --> 00:22:40.960
And then, yeah, basically the
node count that you choose

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:42.710
is what drives the cost.

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:46.300
And again, also, we have egress
cost there that you incur.

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:49.240
Now, how can you
optimize on these levels?

00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:53.260
So one optimization that
is many times overlooked

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:57.830
in Cloud Storage is the
storage classes that you have.

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:00.830
So the storage classes is we
have a multi-region storage,

00:23:00.830 --> 00:23:03.520
we have a regional storage
class, we have nearline,

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:04.840
and we have coldline.

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:07.360
And especially if you go
to nearline and coldline,

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:11.990
the storage cost at
rest goes down a lot.

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:14.440
Now, there are certain
things we can, of course--

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:15.610
storage costs go down.

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:19.960
But we cannot just lower the
prices without giving some

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:21.470
constraints on these.

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:24.940
So if you go to
nearline, we recommend,

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:26.890
if you have data
that is not accessed

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:29.300
more than once a
month, then its worth

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:31.360
to move your data to nearline.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:35.020
If you access it more
than once a month, then

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:37.330
you'll actually incur
more costs because we

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:41.680
give a little bit of penalty
for retrieval of data

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:44.580
if you use nearline.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:47.530
Now, coldline is
basically for something

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:50.170
where you want to have
archival or backups

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:52.870
that you don't hope
and expect to use.

00:23:52.870 --> 00:23:55.660
So coldline is-- or
something for legal reasons

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:57.490
that you have to
store for a long time.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:24:01.640
Coldline is if you access
data less than once a year.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:05.160
So if you access data once
a year or twice a year,

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:07.030
coldline is already
more expensive

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:10.250
than a regional instance.

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:12.820
Now, the really cool thing
in Google Cloud platform

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:18.540
for Google Cloud Storage is that
these storage classes are not--

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:20.130
you can define buckets.

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:22.950
So you can define buckets
where your objects go in,

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:26.280
and then all of your data
that goes into that bucket

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:27.810
gets that storage class.

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:30.000
But that's not the only
way to have storage classes

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:31.170
for your objects.

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:33.920
You can actually use one bucket
and have different storage

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:36.730
classes for the
object in a bucket.

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:39.990
And so there's something
called object lifecycle.

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:41.820
And for the object
lifecycle, you

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:45.240
have the option, for instance,
say, OK, if that object is not

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:47.370
accessed for,
let's say, 30 days,

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:48.900
please move it to nearline.

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:52.290
If it hasn't been
accessed for 365 days,

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:55.050
please move it to coldline.

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:56.850
And you can define
these policies,

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:58.930
and this is done
automatically for you.

00:24:58.930 --> 00:25:00.000
So you don't have to--

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.440
you define these policies once.

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:03.640
And then you say,
I'm off the hook.

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:05.790
Over time, the data
that I'm storing,

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.130
it's going to be getting
cheaper for me, right?

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:10.290
So you don't have to keep
that at top of the mind.

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:15.150
OK, I have to run a run job that
basically moves these objects.

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:19.860
Now, another thing that you can
do that I did in a demo at I/O

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:22.980
is, for instance, if you have
certain blobs that come in,

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:25.875
like videos, or images,
or something like that,

00:25:25.875 --> 00:25:30.540
that you do a processing of-- so
you do, for instance, generate

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:34.590
thumbnails, or you do
re-decoding of your videos

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:37.350
or things like that, and you
want to keep the raw image

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:40.180
or raw video footage around,
but you know you're not actually

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:43.440
serving that very often,
you can, right after you

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:46.440
process your data,
change the storage

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:47.969
class of that raw object.

00:25:47.969 --> 00:25:50.510
And you basically say, OK, I'm
processing the data right now.

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:52.560
Once the last
process is finished,

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:54.632
I move it to coldline.

00:25:54.632 --> 00:25:55.590
And you're done, right?

00:25:55.590 --> 00:26:00.460
So you can do that with client
libraries and our APIs as well.

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:03.770
So you can do that
programmatically.

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:07.130
Now, for Firestore, what I
recommend to basically keep

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:11.390
costs under control,
look at using limits,

00:26:11.390 --> 00:26:15.890
like how much data you get
returned from your queries.

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:17.690
Look at cursors and offset.

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:20.030
So basically, you
can use a cursor

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:23.690
if you do paging that
you only really get

00:26:23.690 --> 00:26:26.390
the data into your application
and presentation layer

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:29.800
that you're actually serving to
your customer or to your user.

00:26:29.800 --> 00:26:33.164
Don't get all the data
and do some processing.

00:26:33.164 --> 00:26:35.330
If you do that every time,
you incur basically a lot

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:37.250
of costs on these operations.

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:39.560
So we have costs
attached to reads,

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:43.940
writes, and delete
operations on Firestore.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:46.790
And another recommendation
is to do analytics outside.

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:50.660
So consider, if you store data
and you use it operationally

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:52.710
in Firestore for
your application,

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:54.950
if you do want to do
analytics over your data,

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:59.330
consider moving that data out,
for instance, into BigQuery.

00:26:59.330 --> 00:27:03.380
For Cloud SQL, things
to basically look out

00:27:03.380 --> 00:27:06.720
for keeping costs under
control, use the right size,

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:08.180
the right instant size.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:10.310
Of course, do a
projection where you

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:12.750
can grow so that you don't
have to change the instance

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:16.850
size every month, and how
your traffic pattern is.

00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:19.370
But the instance size is
basically your granularity

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:22.520
that you can choose to
have costs under control.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:26.495
And then you have things like
failover and read replicas.

00:27:26.495 --> 00:27:29.440
So don't just right away
create a read replica

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:30.510
if you don't need it.

00:27:30.510 --> 00:27:33.770
So there are certain
capabilities of your Cloud SQL

00:27:33.770 --> 00:27:36.530
instance out of the box
where you don't necessarily

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:37.930
need the read replica.

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:41.690
So read replica comes in
if you hit, basically,

00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:44.540
the boundaries of your
Cloud SQL instance in terms

00:27:44.540 --> 00:27:46.490
of write and read throughput.

00:27:46.490 --> 00:27:49.160
If you want to get a little bit
higher in the write throughput,

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:50.960
you can consider a
read replica, which

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:55.320
will take away a little bit
of the work from your master,

00:27:55.320 --> 00:27:56.810
or leader node.

00:27:56.810 --> 00:28:00.530
And then you can, with that,
get a little bit more headroom

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:04.950
in the write throughput
on your instance.

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:07.730
Now, for Cloud
Spanner, we recommend

00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:10.460
to scale depending on your
traffic, on your workload,

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:11.720
basically.

00:28:11.720 --> 00:28:14.330
So do some tests,
watch how your traffic

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:16.400
is for your application.

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:19.550
And you are charged
on a granularity

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:21.740
of an hour on Cloud Spanner.

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:25.170
So don't do scaling
every 10 minutes.

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.590
So basically, do a
little bit, like--

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:29.070
space the scaling
a little bit out.

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:31.860
So you pay for a node,
anyway, for an hour.

00:28:31.860 --> 00:28:34.820
So consider that if
you program any kind

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:38.150
of scaling for your
Cloud Spanner instance.

00:28:38.150 --> 00:28:43.128
All right, number four
is logs and analytics.

00:28:43.128 --> 00:28:45.450
And before I'm going to
go in logs and analytics,

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:46.970
I want to show really quick--

00:28:46.970 --> 00:28:48.890
I mentioned Cloud Firestore.

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:50.810
And I want to show a
little demo, at least,

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:51.650
for Cloud Firestore.

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:56.250
So I'm going to switch
to my laptop here.

00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:59.530
And some of you might
have seen it already.

00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:01.510
We have it as a showcase demo.

00:29:01.510 --> 00:29:04.890
So basically, what I wanted to
show with Cloud Firestore is

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:07.770
it's very easy to keep
multiple applications

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:10.050
on different platform--
no, an application

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:13.150
on different platforms
and devices in sync,

00:29:13.150 --> 00:29:15.150
so we allow something
that you can watch

00:29:15.150 --> 00:29:17.430
for data changes in Firestore.

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:21.620
This is a feature that we had
for the real-time database

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:23.700
under Firebase.

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:26.240
And so what you see here
is basically the console

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:29.390
for Firestore under Firebase.

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:31.350
And you see here we
have a database one.

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:32.260
We have some data.

00:29:32.260 --> 00:29:35.370
We have some rules, basically
who can access the data.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:39.660
You can define indexes or
remove indexes if you want to.

00:29:39.660 --> 00:29:42.990
And you can see the usage,
basically, of how much data

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:44.310
we're processing.

00:29:44.310 --> 00:29:46.200
Now, in this demo,
what I wanted to show

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:49.740
is the scalability that is
possible with Firestore.

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:54.500
So the demo is I have devices
which are watching a document.

00:29:54.500 --> 00:29:56.750
So I'm going to show you,
really quick, this document.

00:29:56.750 --> 00:29:58.800
You have basically
here a document

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:00.150
that can change a color.

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:03.510
Every time I change a color,
10,000 imitated devices

00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:05.190
are catching that data change.

00:30:05.190 --> 00:30:07.740
They get basically notified
about the changing color.

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:09.960
You just saw it changing.

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:12.480
They get notified about
the changing color.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:15.090
And once they get that, they
write that back, actually,

00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:15.960
to Firestore.

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:20.207
So this color that you see
here is in my import document.

00:30:20.207 --> 00:30:21.540
So I have four import documents.

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:23.310
I split up, basically,
the Firestore logo

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:25.300
into four pieces.

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:28.080
And then you see here a
color and a timestamp.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:33.010
And every time we see a
change, these devices report

00:30:33.010 --> 00:30:34.510
that back to the database.

00:30:34.510 --> 00:30:36.990
Now, how do I want
to visualize that?

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:38.790
The way that I'm
visualizing this is,

00:30:38.790 --> 00:30:43.320
actually, you have here on the
left side the Firestore logo.

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:45.450
And each pixel,
each color pixel,

00:30:45.450 --> 00:30:47.520
is represented by one device.

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:49.290
Now, if I change the
color you see here,

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:53.880
the color change gets basically
propagated to these 10,000

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:55.840
devices per section.

00:30:55.840 --> 00:30:57.900
In total, we have
40,000 devices.

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:00.570
Every time a color changes,
they get basically propagated,

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:03.450
and then I display
that in my display app.

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:07.920
And I didn't have to do anything
for that in terms of looking,

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:10.620
how do I notify my devices
or things like that?

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:12.120
I just used the
client libraries,

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:14.130
and all this is done
automatically for you.

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:19.710
The next thing that I wanted
to talk about a little bit

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:21.540
is logs and analytics.

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:26.400
So we have a couple of products
there that help you to analyze

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:29.880
your usage patterns, your
data that you are collecting,

00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:32.550
that you want to analyze
to, for instance,

00:31:32.550 --> 00:31:35.460
machine learning to get
insights into your business,

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:40.150
to find out what works
reliably and what doesn't.

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:43.060
So the products of my
choice here in that area

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:47.100
are BigQuery, which is
basically an OLAP relational

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:50.070
database for massive
scale data analytics.

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:52.645
And I'll show a little
demo in a second.

00:31:52.645 --> 00:31:54.210
The second one is
Cloud DataFlow.

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:59.830
Cloud DataFlow is a data
processing infrastructure

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:03.480
and, basically,
platform where we

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:06.660
have a library or, basically,
a programming model

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:10.230
that we open sourced under the
Apache Beam umbrella, which

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:13.090
allows you to write data
processing pipelines that work

00:32:13.090 --> 00:32:16.050
always in streaming mode
and in benching mode, which

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:17.290
is very powerful.

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:19.290
And then if you have
already any kind

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:21.800
of existing data
analytic pipelines,

00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:25.920
be it in Apache Hadoop
or in Apache Spark,

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:29.800
you can run these, basically,
and migrate them, basically,

00:32:29.800 --> 00:32:34.560
in a lift and shift manner and
run them on Cloud Dataproc.

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:36.870
So now, if we look
at all these metrics,

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:40.470
again, in terms of scalability,
reliability, and performance--

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:46.290
performance, well, they are
not end user infrastructure

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:47.280
products that you use.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:50.760
You don't use these products
to serve something directly

00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:51.900
to your users.

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:56.970
So don't use BigQuery
directly connected

00:32:56.970 --> 00:32:58.070
into your application.

00:32:58.070 --> 00:33:00.360
If you use BigQuery,
please cache--

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:03.030
do some caching on top of
it, otherwise your users

00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:04.710
have to wait a
little bit of time.

00:33:04.710 --> 00:33:08.750
So query run times in BigQuery
are usually at least a second,

00:33:08.750 --> 00:33:09.420
right?

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:13.890
This is not a real-time,
fast answer query machine.

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:17.120
And that's just, by the nature,
how BigQuery is designed.

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:20.450
Now, for Cloud DataFlow,
it's pretty similar.

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:24.630
For data going through DataFlow,
it incurs some latencies.

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:26.940
So you don't want to
rely on that, either.

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:29.820
Basically, these
are async systems

00:33:29.820 --> 00:33:31.470
to use in the async matter.

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:34.200
And then Cloud Dataproc, as
mentioned, is a system for you

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:38.280
that you can use Apache
Hadoop or Apache Spark on it.

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.610
And they're, in terms of
scalability and performance,

00:33:41.610 --> 00:33:45.210
all built on Google Cloud
Platform on our infrastructure.

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:47.470
And we make sure that
these are up and running.

00:33:47.470 --> 00:33:50.910
Especially, if you
look at BigQuery,

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:52.950
they are running on
so many machines.

00:33:52.950 --> 00:33:56.610
Even if a machine falls down,
another one picks up the work.

00:33:56.610 --> 00:33:58.920
And it's similar
for Cloud DataFlow.

00:33:58.920 --> 00:34:02.230
Now, what are the cost
drivers for these systems?

00:34:02.230 --> 00:34:05.880
If you look at BigQuery,
it's basically scanned data,

00:34:05.880 --> 00:34:10.080
or what your query
touches in terms of data.

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:13.100
Storage, which is very
reasonably priced,

00:34:13.100 --> 00:34:17.940
it's pretty much priced on
nearline and coldline prices

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:19.290
for Cloud Storage.

00:34:19.290 --> 00:34:22.650
So you can actually keep
your data in BigQuery,

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:25.290
and you don't have to move
it out into Cloud Storage

00:34:25.290 --> 00:34:27.280
or move it back in.

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:29.489
BigQuery has also
federation mode.

00:34:29.489 --> 00:34:31.679
So you can actually
use data that

00:34:31.679 --> 00:34:34.739
is directly stored in Cloud
Storage and query that data.

00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:38.179
Now, we don't have the data
storage optimizations there.

00:34:38.179 --> 00:34:41.400
So queries will be
a little bit slower.

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:43.870
But you can, if you
want to, directly

00:34:43.870 --> 00:34:47.760
query data that is
laying in Cloud Storage.

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:52.400
Now, for DataFlow, you
pay for per virtual CPU.

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:55.290
So you pay a GCE instance.

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:58.530
And then the virtual CPU,
you pay how much memory

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:00.390
you want to probation for that.

00:35:00.390 --> 00:35:02.280
You pay for the
storage that you use

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:04.860
and the data that you
process, basically.

00:35:04.860 --> 00:35:07.542
We have some cool
services on the DataFlow.

00:35:07.542 --> 00:35:09.000
So we have a shuffle
service, which

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:11.940
is outside of your
instances, which

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:15.270
uses a novel way of
shuffling your data,

00:35:15.270 --> 00:35:18.180
which can free up a lot
of the size requirements

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:19.160
on your instance.

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:20.610
So if you use our
shuffle service,

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:23.430
you need smaller instances
to process your data.

00:35:23.430 --> 00:35:25.320
So you can save on
that and then you

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:27.630
pay us a little bit for the
shuffling service, which

00:35:27.630 --> 00:35:30.880
is also more
performant and faster.

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:35.760
On Cloud Dataproc,
basically GCE instances

00:35:35.760 --> 00:35:38.130
that you use-- so you can
use the instances that we

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:39.040
have available.

00:35:39.040 --> 00:35:42.460
You can also customize your
instances for your pipelines

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:43.650
that you want to use.

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:46.710
And you pay a premium,
a Dataproc premium,

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:52.800
on top of this per
CPU, I believe.

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:56.970
And what we charge is the
minimum of one minute,

00:35:56.970 --> 00:35:59.334
but we charge it per second.

00:35:59.334 --> 00:36:00.750
And then the choice
is, basically,

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:04.950
what you have for saving costs,
if you look right away on Cloud

00:36:04.950 --> 00:36:09.240
Dataproc, is, one, you
can use preemptible VMs.

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:14.040
So if your jobs allow you,
basically, that machines

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:16.240
become unavailable
if you re-claim them

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:20.290
and your job can pick up
the work from a machine

00:36:20.290 --> 00:36:23.200
that we reclaim, then you
can use preemptible VMs.

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:25.660
And preemptible VMs are
considerably cheaper

00:36:25.660 --> 00:36:29.980
than GCE instances that
you basically can keep.

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:34.420
So consider that to optimizing
your costs on the analytics

00:36:34.420 --> 00:36:35.230
side.

00:36:35.230 --> 00:36:37.630
For Cloud DataFlow, you
have the possibility

00:36:37.630 --> 00:36:41.490
to provision Standard
Persistant Disk, or SSD.

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:45.580
So SSD is also
considerably more higher

00:36:45.580 --> 00:36:47.850
priced than standard PDs.

00:36:47.850 --> 00:36:49.960
So that's also
something to consider

00:36:49.960 --> 00:36:54.020
based on your requirements
off how fast your analytics

00:36:54.020 --> 00:36:56.060
pipeline have to run.

00:36:56.060 --> 00:36:58.300
So that's something that
you should test, basically,

00:36:58.300 --> 00:36:59.890
for your workloads,
what is cheaper.

00:36:59.890 --> 00:37:03.090
If you run with SSD,
then maybe your pipelines

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:05.230
are shorter in the
time that they need.

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:09.300
And you can pay less
for the instance costs

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:11.680
but a little bit more
for the persistent disk.

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:16.300
So that's something that you
should check on your workloads.

00:37:16.300 --> 00:37:20.800
And for Big Query, what we
recommend for optimization

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:23.770
is run your queries on
a small dataset first.

00:37:23.770 --> 00:37:27.460
Make sure that your queries are
optimized on a small data set.

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:29.660
Don't run them on
your entire data set.

00:37:29.660 --> 00:37:33.250
You will incur a lot
of costs every time.

00:37:33.250 --> 00:37:34.500
So run it on a small data set.

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:39.310
Optimize them that they read
as few of data as possible.

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:42.090
And then you can run them
across your entire data

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:45.130
set that you want to do.

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:47.290
And then another
option is you have

00:37:47.290 --> 00:37:50.500
the choice between on-demand and
flat rate billing for BigQuery.

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:54.205
So consider-- if you are running
a lot of BigQuery queries

00:37:54.205 --> 00:37:57.700
or jobs, then consider,
too, our flat rate

00:37:57.700 --> 00:38:02.020
pricing, which can help you
as well to save some cost.

00:38:02.020 --> 00:38:09.640
